    "# Sequentially valid tests for forecast calibration\n",
    "The original papers argues that the existing tools for forecast validation do not fully account for the sequential nature of forecasting. The classical tools for validating calibration require the sample size to be fixed in advance and independently of the data. To illustrate this limitation, the authors present the example of a weather forecaster evaluating daily probabilistic forecasts over one year. If the forecaster notices midway through the year that the forecasts exhibit strong bias, applying a classical goodness-of-fit test using all data collected up to that point would yield an invalid p-value, as the sample size is no longer independent of the data since stopping is based on the observed data. On the other hand, if the forecaster waits until the end of the year to analyze the data, the PIT distribution for the entire period might still appear uniform. This could happen if the bias reverses direction in the second half of the year, masking the misspecification and preventing its detection.\n"
   ]
  },
