    "## Parametric setting\n",
    "---\n",
    "In the parametric setting, the paper suggest testing the null against the family of beta distributions $B(\\alpha,\\beta)$ where any of the probability measures $P_{(\\alpha,\\beta)} \\in \\mathcal{H}_1$ can be expressed by $\\Theta = \\{(\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 | \\alpha >0,\\beta >0\\}$. Note that $P_{(1,1)} \\sim UNIF(0,1)$ with a probability density function value of 1 for all $ x \\in [0,1]$. The null is therefore a *simple hypothesis* and for any $(\\alpha,\\beta) \\neq (1,1)$ the likelihood ratio \n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "    E^{\\alpha,\\beta}(Z_F) := \\frac{1}{B(\\alpha,\\beta)} Z_F^{\\alpha-1}(1-Z_F)^{\\beta -1}\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "is an e-value testing $\\mathcal{H}_0$ against $\\mathcal{H}_1 :=\\{P_{(\\alpha,\\beta)}\\}$. A natural method of determining the value of $\\alpha,\\beta$ in a sequential setting is maximum likelihood estimation based on a sequence of observations $(z_t)_{t\\in \\mathbb{N}}$\n",
    "\\begin{equation*}\n",
    "    (\\hat{\\alpha},\\hat{\\beta})_t = \\underset{(\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\Theta}{\\text{argmax}} \\sum_{i=1}^{t} \\log(p_{(\\alpha,\\beta)}(z_i))\n",
    "\\end{equation*}\n",
    "\n",
    "---"
   ]
  },
